🔧 **F-1666-2 (s1666 2026-08-11, MEASURED + CURED THE SAME FIRE — THE DESK-CARRYFORWARD GUARD IS FULLY BUILT, CORRECT, TEETH-PROVEN, GRANDFATHERED AS "CALLED BY A LAW" — AND NOTHING HAS EVER RUN IT AGAINST THE LIVE BOARD. ITS FIRST MEASURED CASUALTY IS THE FIRE IMMEDIATELY BEFORE ME. NOT AN OWNER QUESTION.)** 📋 **HOW IT SURFACED, which matters because nobody was looking for it:** §4 tells a fire to run `desk-state-audit --status` on what it INHERITED. I did. It returned **1 item** (`F-1659-1`, OPEN-DESK-ONLY) — while s1665's own line-1 header declares **"OWNER'S DESK — 21 awaiting a word"** and visibly lists 21 ids. **A 20-item discrepancy between a desk's own header and the instrument that reads it.** 🎯 **ROOT CAUSE — AN ENCODING CHANGE, NOT A DROP.** `deskItems()` (`desk-carryforward-guard.mjs:152`) splits the desk tail on **🔺** and takes the first id in each segment's `KEY_ZONE`; the convention is therefore **one 🔺 per item**. **s1664's line-1 carries 22 markers → parses as 21 items ✓. s1665's carries 2** — one header, then all 21 ids run together under a single 🔺 separated by `·` — **so it parses as 1.** s1665 did **not** drop anything and its count of 21 was honestly obtained (it ran the audit on s1664's line and reported `OPEN-DESK-ONLY=21` faithfully); **it wrote a correct desk in a format its own reader cannot see.** ⚠️ **THE CONSEQUENCE IS THE SILENT-DROP CLASS, ONE FIRE FROM HAPPENING:** the next fire doing exactly what §4 orders reads **one** item and carries **one** forward, retiring twenty owner-desk items that no owner ever ruled on — including `F-E2S-3`, `F-DOOR-6` and `F-1101-1`. That is precisely the s1527 event (`ten dropped, incl. "13 of 25 campaign maps cannot be opened"`) that `desk-carryforward-guard` was BUILT to prevent. 🔍 **AND THE GUARD CATCHES IT PERFECTLY — I PROVED IT BY MANUFACTURING, NOT BY A GREEN (the s1299/s1300 standard).** Reconstructed s1665's exact post-handoff `STATUS.md` in `/tmp/s1666-deskrepro` (its handoff as line-1, its own