🟢 **F-1661-4 (s1661 2026-08-11, MEASURED + CURED THIS FIRE — TWO REFUSALS ON THE REFILL SURFACE PRINT A HEAD THAT INVERTS THEIR VERDICT: THE TOOL SHOWS THE WORD "UNBLOCKED" FOR WORK THAT IS STILL REFUSED. FACTORY-OPS, NOT AN OWNER QUESTION.)** 🧭 **HOW I FOUND IT: by nearly making the mistake.** The board was dry, both lanes busy, so I went to the refill surface for the idle lanes. `authorable-candidates.mjs` prints `bt-04-homestead-automation` and `e3-fairground-socket` as **PROSE-ONLY**, and the ~200-char window it shows of each reason opens: *"**UNBLOCKED** (owner greenlight 2026-08-09, desk walkthrough — all three chosen) — prior hold kept as history: …"*. **Read at the speed a refill is actually done, that is an invitation** — an owner greenlight, dated, with the refusal explicitly labelled *history*. ✗ **BOTH ARE STILL REFUSED, AND NEITHER REFUSAL APPEARS IN THE PRINTED WINDOW.** `bt-04`: the roundup greenlit the **program**, not the **parameters** — its declaring row (grep by content, F-1352-1) states the residual in its own words, *"A GREENLIT PROGRAM IS NOT AN ANSWER TO FORKS (1)–(4)"*, and names the next act **"attended, not fire"**: put four concrete questions to Robin (what X is in *auto-repair under X%*; what *idle* means and where the Prospector pans; the building-tiers Law 4 balance ruling on an autonomous gold engine; the endless-mode session spine). ✓ I checked whether a fork-free sub-slice exists and it does not — **the four forks between them span BT-04's entire scope.** `e3-fairground`: the roundup's own sentence carries the caveat — *"fairground needs the **ATTENDED ENGINE SLICE** — owed by the attended session, next working pass"* — and until it lands the `HeadlessContractSim` door still refuses `e3-fairground`, so **any master built on it is jointly unsatisfiable**; its existing master already burned **124,606 tokens** and §7.5 is not satisfied by a greenlight (a changed premise for authoring a NEW construction path is not a licence to re-run the one that failed). 🔑 **THE MECHANISM, AND IT IS A WRITING FAULT, NOT A TOOL BUG:** both reasons were **edited in place** when the block lifted — the new state was prepended as a headline and the surviving refusal was left in, or below, the tail. `resolveRefusal` treats any legacy prose key as *priced but unclassified* and the caller prints the head, which is correct behaviour; **what changed underneath it is that the head stopped being the verdict.** ⚠️ **A truncated field is only as safe as its FIRST sentence, so a refusal must lead with the refusal** — the same shape as F-1641-1 (a flip that appends a clause instead of retiring the one it contradicts) and F-1655-3 / F-1659-2 (a bare reader mistaking a disclaimer for a declaration), landing this time on the surface a fire consults *when it is deciding what to build*. ✅ **CURED by pricing both leaves with structured `authoringBlock`s** (class **`attended-owed`** — F-1383-1's discriminator applied to authoring: an attended act, so deliberately **NOT** carried to the owner's desk where it would park forever), each written so its first clause is *"NOT fire-authorable: …"* and each naming the exact act owed. **The legacy prose is retained, not rewritten** (Retention Law) — `resolveRefusal` prefers the structured block, so the history stays readable without steering anyone. **Surface moves PROSE-ONLY 3 → 1, attended-owed 2 → 4.** ⓘ **NOT MECHANISED, deliberately:** a guard asserting "every refusal's first sentence must refuse" is unwriteable without prose-matching, and prose-matching guards get excused into uselessness (the `cross-engine` label's fate, F-1460-1). The durable cure is the structured field, which has no head to invert. 💡 **THE LESSON: when a gate lifts, the surviving refusal must be REWRITTEN AT THE TOP, not appended below the good news.** A record edited in place keeps its old shape and its new headline, and the reader who is skimming gets exactly the wrong half.
🟢 **F-1653-1 (s1653 2026-08-11, FOUND WHILE DISCHARGING F-1652-1's OWED CLAUSE — THE OUTAGE HAD A SIBLING THAT COULD HAVE RECREATED IT WITH NO FIRE INVOLVED. FACTORY-OPS, CURED THIS FIRE — NOTHING HERE IS AN OWNER QUESTION).** 📋 **THE OWED CLAUSE IS DISCHARGED, AND EXECUTABLY RATHER THAN AS PROSE.** F-1652-1 asked for the restart recipe in a law surface plus a `scripts/fire.md` §2.0 line about measuring `tty`/`ppid` before escalating. Both landed: **§2.0b** in `scripts/fire.md`, and **`scripts/start-lane-runner.sh`** — because the thing that failed was a recipe being retyped correctly under pressure at 05:08, so a script that cannot be mistyped beats a paragraph that can. 🔎 **(1) THE SIBLING: `scripts/health-watch.sh` RESTARTS THE RUNNER AUTOMATICALLY, AND IT HAD THE SAME DEFECT.** Its one auto-remediation ran `nohup bash scripts/lane-runner-v3.sh` inheriting **its own** caller's PATH, and its caller is `scripts/com.goldrush.health.plist` — a launchd job with **no `EnvironmentVariables` key at all**, i.e. launchd's bare PATH with no nvm. ⚠️ **So the ~5h03m outage class was reachable with NO fire and NO human in the loop**, on a 600-second timer. ⓘ **It is LATENT, not live, and that is stated rather than glossed: the plist is NOT loaded** — neither `logs/health-launchd.log` nor `logs/runner-headless.log` exists, and `RunAtLoad true` + `StandardOutPath` would have created the first on its very first run. The defect therefore never fired; it was armed and waiting for someone to `launchctl load` it. Now routed through the helper. 🎯 **(2) THE OWED CLAUSE OVERSTATED ONE HALF AND NEVER NAMED THE OTHER — re-measured s1653, not inherited.** It prescribes prepending a floor-meeting nvm bin *"to PATH"* as the cure. But **codex is no longer load-bearing**: s1651's `resolve_codex_bin` (`lane-runner-v3.sh:60`) picks a floor-meeting client **before** the master-consuming `mv`, so dispatch **self-heals** a wrong-shell PATH. ➡️ **What IS still load-bearing is `node`/`npm`/`npx`**, which the finding does not mention: the runner passes `"$codex_bin"` explicitly and **never modifies `PATH`** (`:220`), so every `npm run build` / `npx tsc` / `npx playwright` / `node --test` a lane task runs resolves from the **runner's inherited** PATH. A fire-started runner therefore runs every lane gate under **node v26.4.0** instead of the **v23.11.1** that produced all 13 healthy runs and every gate baseline on the board. **Proven live by `--check`, which flips the child interpreter from v26.4.0 to v23.11.1.** ⚖️ **Whether v26 actually breaks the build is UNMEASURED and is NOT the claim** — the defect is an uncontrolled swap of interpreter underneath the evidence, not a known break. 🚫 **(3) A CORRECTION TO F-1652-1's READING OF THE LOCK, from the code rather than the error text.** F-1652-1 records that the escalation's error message (*"another instance running. Remove if stale."*) invited the harmful path. Reading `lane-runner-v3.sh:13–:18` sharpens it into a positive rule: the runner **already self-heals a genuine corpse** — no other runner process → `rmdir` + retake, automatically. **So a lock that is actually held ALWAYS means a live instance**, and `rmdir tasks/.runner.lock` by hand can only ever produce two runners on one queue set. The helper refuses instead, and points at `kill -TERM` (trap at `:24` releases the lock cleanly, 4.5 s measured s1652). 🔒 **(4) GUARDED, WITH ITS REDS PROVEN BY MANUFACTURING THEM.** `scripts/runner-restart-recipe.test.sh`, rooted in `test:ledger-guards` beside its sibling `codex-client-floor.test.sh`. Six red paths each proven on scratch copies (bare-nohup restored → FAIL · `CLAUDECODE` left set → FAIL · PATH prepend removed → FAIL · live-runner refusal removed → FAIL · helper clearing the lock → FAIL · `CODEX_FLOOR` drift → FAIL), control green — because a passing guard never executes its violation path. It also reds on **floor drift** between helper and runner, the one thing that can silently rot given the helper deliberately does not source the runner (sourcing would execute it; copying it wholesale is the sibling-script hazard this row is about). 💡 **(5) THE GUARD CAUGHT ITS OWN AUTHOR ON ITS FIRST RUN, which is the reusable bit.** The lock check reddened on correct code: the helper *warns* `Do NOT rmdir tasks/.runner.lock` in prose, and a naive `grep` matched the warning. **A probe must see what the shell would EXECUTE, not what the file says about it** — comments and `echo`/`printf` lines are stripped before matching. A guard whose subject is a documented hazard will match its own documentation unless told not to.
