-lookup.test.mjs` 18/18, and both hashes carry the claimed files. All three now read `closed` at their own line. ⚠️ **Cite F-1603-1 in the flipped head if you like, but know why it is safe:** classification is subject-FIRST, so an id merely cited after the row's own id carries no state — **verified as a control this fire, F-1603-1 stayed OPEN across both flips.**

🟢 **F-1600-1** (s1600 2026-08-09, MEASURED AND CURED THIS FIRE, DOC-ONLY — THE GAZETTE SWEEP’S HASH CHECK IS STRUCTURALLY BLIND TO THE SECOND OWNER-FACING SINK, SO 44.7% OF ITS “ABSENT” VERDICTS ARE ITEMS THE OWNER HAS ALREADY BEEN SHOWN). Found while discharging s1599’s deliberately-deferred (B). ⚠️ **THE SENTENCE THIS REFUTES IS A PRIOR FINDING’S EXONERATION.** F-1593-1 audited this very sweep, split it into halves, and wrote *“the hash half is a `grep` and cannot go wrong; the ‘real-change’ half needs the merge’s file list”* — then spent its entire investigation on the second half. **The hash half went wrong in two independent ways, and that exoneration is why nobody looked.** 🔑 **THE MECHANISM:** the news pipeline has TWO owner-facing sinks — GAZETTE items in `marketing/outbox/gazette-queue.md` and the daily TK-01 ticker digests sitting beside it — and the duty’s prescribed check greps ONE file for the FULL 40-char hash. 📐 **The two sinks cite hashes in DISJOINT conventions, which makes the blindness categorical rather than a sampling artifact — ✓ VERIFIED by counting every hash-shaped token in the live outbox: ticker digests carry 618 short hashes and ZERO full-40 ones; `gazette-queue.md` carries 86 full and 105 short.** A full-40 grep therefore **cannot** match a ticker digest in principle, however many fires run it. 📊 **MEASURED (`scripts/tmp-s1600-outbox-sinks.mjs`, window 2026-08-05→s1600): 1065 first-parent commits, 81 touching player paths (`src/` `functions/` `public/` `index.html` `e2e/`), of which the narrow check calls 47 ABSENT — but 20 are headlined in a ticker digest and 1 sits in the gazette under its short hash. So 21 of 47 are FALSE POSITIVES (44.7%), and only 26 are genuinely unreported.** ⚠️ **My denominator is NOT s1599’s, and I do not claim to have re-priced its exact figures:** s1599 filtered on commit-message prefix (53 shaped / 37 absent); I filtered on touched paths (81 / 47). **The transferable result is the RATE, not the count.** ⓘ **THE DAMAGE IS AN ITEM THAT CAN NEVER BE DISCHARGED, NOT WASTED GREPS:** `3b2b33c29` (“the county’s first standing — V4 Pro secures the-claim via the public door”) was left by s1599 as an UNJUDGED gazette candidate and re-judged by me — when it had been headlined to the owner in `ticker-digest-2026-08-06.md:27–29` on 2026-08-07, **carrying its own OWNER CHOICE note at `:58`**, since before either fire existed. A check that reports a discharged duty as owed reports it forever, and every fire pays the judgement cost again; this one was on its third pass. ✅ **VERDICT ON THAT CANDIDATE, so it stops circulating: REPORTED, NOT OWED — no gazette item, and it was never a real-change merge anyway** (its diff is `logs/seed-ladder/`, two run transcripts, `scripts/seed-ladder.mjs` +5/-1, `tasks/BACKLOG.md` +1 — no `src/`, no `functions/`). 💡 **THIS REPRICES ANY ABSENCE COUNT TAKEN THE NARROW WAY, INCLUDING F-1599-1’S** — if ~45% of such a set is already published, the backlog it implies is smaller than it reads, and a cure sized against the raw figure is over-sized. ⚖️ **BUT IT DOES NOT REFUTE F-1599-1, AND I CHECKED RATHER THAN ASSUMED:** its instance `a97ff79c7` appears in the outbox today **only because s1599 backfilled it by hand** (`0760e05da`); re-greped at both hash lengths, **no ticker digest ever carried it**, so F-1599-1 was a TRUE miss and not an artifact of this defect. My finding changes the SIZE of that problem, not its existence. ✅ **CURED IN `scripts/fire.md` GZ-01 THIS FIRE:** the check is now “grep the 8-char SHORT hash across the whole of `marketing/outbox/`”. Doc-only, no code, no new guard — the duty’s own standing prohibition on mechanising this as a red guard is untouched and still right. **GATE: none — closed on landing. Reverse with one word if the owner prefers the narrow check.** ✅ **SWEEP DISCHARGED THIS FIRE WITH THE CORRECTED CHECK, WHICH IS HOW THE REST OF THIS ROW WAS LEARNED.** Of the 47 the narrow check called absent, **4** touch real player source once `e2e/` is dropped from the filter (a test is not a player-facing surface), and **3 of those 4 are genuine misses** — backfilled as one ROUNDUP per the ≤3/week rule: `c8807b4d` (THE FRONT DESK panel beside the county boards — `renderFrontDesk()` + 99 lines of `reader.css`), `7e5fae14` (the e2-trestle terrain rebuild, `Terrain3dClaimPilot.ts` +243 with new atlas/blend/glb), `d9cf6242` (a works with power lines but no connection to make would pin `objectiveAllowsSecure` false forever, so beating the Baron **won the contract and silently failed to secure it**). The 4th, `1a2871fd`, is factory hygiene (composite-key delimiters re-encoded so `grep` works in Claude shells) and is CORRECTLY absent. 🔎 **A THIRD BLINDNESS, found only by discharging the duty rather than measuring it — AND IT IS Mistake #16 SITTING INSIDE THE SWEEP’S OWN FILTER.** s1599 shaped its candidate set by commit-MESSAGE PREFIX (`fix|feat|perf|test|refactor`). **Two of my three genuine misses land under subjects that name something else entirely:** `c8807b4d` ships a whole new player-facing panel under *“bench: prime-agent heat 1 closes DNF×3 (operability trilogy)”*, and `7e5fae14` is a bare *“Merge branch ‘lane/c’ into HEAD”*. **A message-prefix filter cannot see either, and no amount of widening the sink fixes that** — it is the same law the house already wrote after w1-07 (never judge shipped-ness by commit messages), reappearing in the instrument that judges newsworthiness. ➡️ **Shape the candidate set by TOUCHED PATHS, never by subject.** 📐 **The trestle miss also has a sibling control, which is what raised it from “arguably” to “owed”:** `beauty-e2-pressure-garden` (`25890bae…`) and `beauty-e2-incline` (`fa5ed7ca…`) both carry gazette items; the trestle pass is the same class of work by the same hands and had none. Two of three announced is not a judgement call, it is a gap.

🟡 **F-1599-1** (s1599 2026-08-09, OPEN, NON-BLOCKING, DOC-ONLY — THE GAZETTE SWEEP'S FILTER IS BLIND TO THE EXACT PATH F-1546-1 WAS WRITTEN TO CATCH). Found by hand while discharging the standing gazette duty. ⚠️ **THE INSTANCE:** `a97ff79c7` (attended, 2026-08-09 13:53) fixed the front desk's `skill.md` link to be base-aware — before it, a visitor at `agenttown.app/goldrush` clicking the door document was served the landing-teaser's playbook from the site ROOT. That is **plain-boot player-visible** (asserted at `e2e/field-book.spec.ts:173` ("plain boot renders and expands the Minds and Rigs tables" — RE-KEYED s1620: the minds-and-rigs merge `48f217502` RENAMED this test from "…the Field Book matrix" and moved it from :236,241), no `?debug`), it was **owner-found on the release walkthrough**, and it reached main with **no news item**. 🔑 **WHY THE SWEEP COULD NOT SEE IT:** F-1546-1 re-keyed the duty from the DRAINER to the MERGE EVENT — correctly — but left the filter as *"any real-change merge **whose review exists** and whose hash is absent"*. **A review file is a fire-drain artifact.** Attended fixes routinely ship without one, so the re-keyed sweep still cannot see the attended path, which is the very path F-1546-1 was written to cover. The agent was un-keyed; the artifact it produces was not. 📊 **MEASURED, AND THE MEASUREMENT REFUTES THE OBVIOUS CURE — which is the useful half.** Window 2026-08-05→now: **1059 first-parent commits, 53 real-change-shaped** (`fix|feat|perf|test|refactor` prefix), **16 in the gazette, 37 absent** — and of the 37 absent, **27 have no review file**. ⚠️ **Do NOT read "37 misses":** nearly all are factory/infrastructure cures (guard fixes, dashboard classifiers, gate-battery flags, desk auditors, law-pointer re-bases) that are **correctly** absent, exactly as the duty predicts when it forbids mechanising this as a red guard. So the review-file filter is **doing real work** — it is what keeps the sweep's noise survivable — and dropping it would produce the excused-into-uselessness guard the duty already warns about (the `cross-engine` fate, F-1460-1). ➡️ **THEREFORE THE CURE IS NOT A WIDER GREP.** The honest options are (a) a WARN-level list of review-less `fix|feat` merges touching `src/**` for a fire to eyeball — the duty permits WARN and forbids RED; (b) ask attended sessions to leave a one-line review stub on player-visible fixes, which restores the filter's premise rather than removing it; (c) accept the gap and rely on the hand sweep. **Recommend (b), it is the cheapest and it fixes the cause.** ⓘ **ONE CANDIDATE LEFT UNJUDGED, deliberately:** `3b2b33c29` ("the county's first standing — V4 Pro secures the-claim via the public door", 2026-08-06) is absent and genuinely newsworthy, but its diff is logs + seeder script, not a change to what the player sees — a milestone rather than a real-change merge. It is 3 days old and the three-a-week rule applies, so it is a judgement for the next fire or the owner, not a unilateral call at the tail of this one. **GATE: an attended ruling on (a)/(b)/(c) — doc-only either way, no code.**

🟢 **F-1598-1** (s1598 2026-08-09, CURED THIS FIRE — THE RELEASE GATE RAN `build:release` BARE, SO THE E1-LEAK CHECK NEVER EXECUTED AND THE RELEASE SHIPPED PAST AN `rc=1` TRANSCRIPT). Cured in `2d2d55ef8`. ⚙️ **WHAT WAS WRONG:** the 2026-08-09 RELEASE GATE battery invoked `npm run build:release` through `scripts/gate-battery.mjs` with no `--env`, so the release assertion threw on its `GR_RELEASE=e1` precondition — the leaf that proves no E2–E10 content leaked into the public build **never ran**. The battery recorded `SUMMARY {"tsc":0,"build":0,"build:release":1,"core-e2e":0}` / `OVERALL rc=1`, the deploy proceeded, and the ledger row `2cca8861c` called the release *live and verified* — leaving a **red transcript as the release’s only evidence file**. The house form has been written down since F-RB-1: `GR_RELEASE=e1 npm run build:release`; the transcript has no `env+` line, which is the proof it was not used. ✅ **THE RELEASE ITSELF IS SOUND, AND I MEASURED IT RATHER THAN ASSUMING IT:** re-running the assertion against the SHIPPED `dist/` returned **1883 files, 111,070,567 bytes, zero later manifest ids or plate/GLB assets, checked against a non-empty 262-stem denominator** (`artifacts/s1598-release-recheck.txt`). ⚠️ **But that is luck, not the gate — a genuine leak would have sailed through the same hole.** 🔧 **CURE AT THE DRIVER, NOT THE CALLER:** a job whose argv names `build:release` or `assert-release-build.mjs` without `GR_RELEASE=e1` in the RESOLVED environment is now **MISUSE → rc=2, nothing measured**, matching the driver’s existing fail-closed handling of a bad `--cwd`/`--env`. 🚫 **DELIBERATELY NOT CURED BY DEFAULTING THE VARIABLE:** that would silently satisfy the precondition the guard exists to enforce, and would break the standing positive control asserting that a bare invocation is still refused. ✅ **PROVED BY MANUFACTURING THE DEFECT** (the s1299/s1300 standard): with the CLI check removed the new refusal test FAILS; cured file restored **byte-identical**, sha256 `769f8c10`; `gate-battery` **17/17**. 🩹 **AND THE FIRST DRAFT OF THE GUARD WAS ITSELF THE WORSE BUG, RECORDED RATHER THAN SMOOTHED AWAY:** its refusal arm spawned the REAL `npm run build:release`, which in the pre-fix arm executed for **15.2 s and overwrote the repo’s `dist/`** — the very bytes that had just been deployed and verified. A guard that clobbers a release artifact every time it runs is worse than the defect it guards. The arms now use an `echo build:release` stub (97 ms, touches nothing), and `dist/` was rebuilt in the lawful release form, reproducing the shipped bundle down to the hashed entry `index-Bxqf5Fn--diet-a9d5c9a0.js`. 💡 **A FALSE FINDING I DID NOT FILE, because the check was one command:** `scripts/deploy.sh` builds with the PLAIN `npm run build`, which reads like the same hole one layer down — but `vite.config.ts` sets the public base to `GR_BASE` **only when `releaseE1` is true**, and the shipped `index.html` carries `/goldrush/`, so that build provably had `GR_RELEASE=e1`. `deploy.sh` (relative-base pages.dev) and `deploy-alias.sh` (the `/goldrush/` alias, which sets both variables) are two coherent paths, not a defect. **GATE: none — mechanism landed.**

🔴 **F-GNT-4b (2026-08-09, THE REDO REFUTED THE CURE — PATH ISOLATION CANNOT SEAL AN EXAM AGAINST AN UNSANDBOXED LOCAL AGENT).** The isolated-arena re-run of openclaw×pressure-garden produced Sol's hash AGAIN (`fd92780d`, 131s): OpenClaw's `agent --local` exec is unsandboxed — it reads the whole disk, and one `..`-level of nesting only moved the answer two hops away. TRUTH, generalized: while solved arenas/the field book exist on the same machine, EVERY local-exec entrant (openclaw, pi, hermes, prime) is environmentally open-book-capable; behavior, not enforcement, kept prior rows clean. ✅ The WORKING control is the one that fired twice: the examiner's hash-collision audit at the gate (duplicate eventLogHash across entrants = copied play, REJECT). Adopted as protocol: every board post now checks its hash against all stored rows for the same contract+seed. 🔧 True sealing (post-release fork): per-entrant OS sandbox (separate user / container) or a clean exam host with no history. For the release table: the openclaw×pressure-garden cell reads "unsealable on this rig — no verdict"; both rejected rows preserved as `.rejected*`. **GATE: post-release — exam-host decision; the hash audit is in force NOW.** ⚖️ OWNER RATIFIED 2026-08-09 (verbatim: "Maybe we have to start sandboxing the contenders in the gauntlet? ... if the model does not understand the answer and can beat other levels, it is similar to the players being able to cheat. They are cheating themselves in the end. And a copied answer cannot beat the original in the ladder, right?") — sandboxing confirmed as the direction (per-entrant OS user/container or exam host), and the owner's reading of the hash audit is CORRECT and now canonical: a duplicate eventLogHash for the same contract+seed can never displace the original row; copiers gain nothing and pollute their own notebooks. Diet labels handle strategy-studying (different hash); the audit handles copied play (same hash).

🟢 **F-1597-1** (s1597 2026-08-09, CURED THIS FIRE — A BLOCKED GOAL LEAF WITH NO `taskFile` WAS INVISIBLE TO THE §3.0 DRAIN GUARD, SO AN OWNER FORK COULD BE QUEUED WITHOUT A WORD). Cured in `e3605cbdd`. ⚙️ **WHAT WAS WRONG:** `scripts/drain-block-check.mjs` collected goal nodes with `typeof node.taskFile === "string"` — the test that distinguishes a LEAF from a parent goal — and thereby dropped a leaf that is blocked and simply has no master authored yet. **MEASURED, NOT INFERRED:** `tasks/goals.json` holds **5** `status:"blocked"` leaves; `--all` reported **4**. The invisible one is `bt-04-homestead-automation`, whose own `blockedReason` carries **four unanswered owner design forks** and ends **"DO NOT QUEUE without a fresh owner ruling on all four forks"** — and whose title says it was **"REGISTERED AS BLOCKED s1354 for Goal Registration Law visibility ONLY"**, so machine visibility was the single thing the registration was for and the one thing it did not buy. 🎯 **WHY IT MATTERED TODAY, NOT HYPOTHETICALLY:** this fire arrived on a DRY board with four idle lanes, which is exactly the state §2E answers by authoring from a live ladder — and the BT ladder is live (CLAUDE.md §9). A fire reaching for BT-04 would have run the §3.0 lookup and been told nothing. **PRE-FIX, ALL THREE SPELLINGS:** `lane-bt-04-homestead-automation.md` → **rc=0 `? UNKNOWN`**; `bt-04-homestead-automation.md --queue` → **rc=0 `? UNKNOWN`**; bare id → same. §3.0 states of exactly this shape that a fire branching on the exit code "reads UNKNOWN as permission — the precise failure §3.0 exists to prevent". ✅ **CURED AT THE COLLECTOR, NOT THE INSTANCE** (F-1248-1s class one layer down — there the audits VOCABULARY was too narrow, here its INPUT was): an unkeyed blocked leaf is collected and matched by its `id`, with the parent-goal exclusion made explicit (a container carries a child array) rather than incidental. Registering a fork BEFORE its master exists stays lawful — that is how a fork gets banked; what stops is a leaf claiming a reachability it never had. **POST-FIX:** `--all` **460 leaves / 5 BLOCKED**, all three spellings **rc=1** naming `blockClass=owner-fork`. 🔒 **TIED DOWN SO THE DENOMINATORS CANNOT DRIFT APART AGAIN:** two assertions added to `scripts/block-class-guard.test.mjs` — "every blocked leaf is visible to the §3.0 drain guard (denominator parity)" and "every blocked leaf refuses the drain under the name a fire would type". That file already walked the tree WITHOUT the taskFile filter, so it had always seen all 5 and passed them (bt-04 declares a valid blockClass) — **two guards over one tree, two denominators, nothing comparing them, and the board read green.** ⚠️ **THE GENERAL LESSON, worth more than the instance: a guard is only as good as its denominator, and when two guards share a subject they must be tied together or they drift silently.** ✅ **PROVED BY MANUFACTURING THE DEFECT, not by a green:** the pre-fix script was installed in place and both new tests FAILED (`but drain-block-check --all reports 4`; `exited 0`), then the cured file was restored **byte-identical** (sha256 `c82c78eeb2491b81`). block-class-guard **5/5 pass**. **NO OWNER ACTION — this is a mechanism fix. It does NOT touch the BT-04 fork itself, which remains owner-gated on its four questions.**

🟢 **F-1596-1** (s1596 2026-08-09, CURED s1599 2026-08-09 in `58a8c3599` — AN OWNER RULING’S ONLY TEST WAS HOUSED UNDER A TITLE THAT DID NOT NAME IT). Found at the `team-and-training` drain (merged `f18879c9ee1bbc1f79439d48cec9b45b4798152d`). ✓ **THE COVERAGE IS REAL AND WAS READ, NOT ASSUMED:** `e2e/field-book.spec.ts:73` ("the Drill Yard is the training ground — standings refuse it on both POST and GET" — RE-KEYED s1620: the minds-and-rigs merge `48f217502` restructured this file and the previously cited title no longer exists) asserts the drill-yard POST returns `400`, the exact `training_ground` error and the house-voice message, that **KV is not written** (`standings:epoch-1-frontier:e1-drill-yard` is null), **and** that the GET path refuses too — both halves of the ruling, green desktop+390px. ⚠️ **THE DEFECT IS WHERE IT LIVES, NOT WHETHER IT WORKS.** Those assertions are appended to an existing test titled *"optional cost fields group the best score by model and never change county ranking"* — a title about something else entirely. So the only test defending an **owner ruling** ("the Drill Yard is not a contract that needs a ladder") is **invisible to a grep for "drill yard" among test names**, and would be deleted silently along with its host if the cost-field test is ever retired. 💡 *Reusable shape: coverage inherits the discoverability of its CONTAINER, not of its assertions. A test title is the only index anyone searches; assertions buried under an unrelated title are protected by nothing but the memory of whoever wrote them.* ➡️ **CURE: split the drill-yard assertions into their own `test(...)` naming the Drill Yard and the refusal.** One-line split, spec file only, no production code. **GATE: none — fold into the next task that opens `e2e/field-book.spec.ts` rather than spending a lane run on it** (s1596 deliberately authored no corrective: the dispatch cost exceeds the fix). ✅ **CURED s1599 `58a8c3599`** — the drill-yard assertions now sit under their own `test('the Drill Yard is the training ground — standings refuse it on both POST and GET')` at `e2e/field-book.spec.ts:72`, with the owner ruling quoted verbatim in a comment above it so the title's reason survives the next reader. The cost-fields test keeps its own fresh `makeKv()` and is unchanged in substance. **Gates:** `tsc --noEmit` clean; `field-book.spec.ts` **8/8** (was 6/6 — the split adds one test × two projects) desktop+390px at `--workers=1`, gated in a DETACHED worktree per §3.0b because an attended Cowork session was live. ✅ **PROVED BY MANUFACTURING THE DEFECT, not by a green** (s1299/s1300 standard): disabling the refusal at `functions/api/standings.ts:333` by file edit turned the extracted test **RED by name**, so it bites rather than passing vacuously; the file was restored and `git status` confirms it byte-identical. ⚠️ **THE LESSON IS THE TRIGGER, NOT THE FIX.** s1596 deliberately deferred this to "the next task that opens `e2e/field-book.spec.ts`" — and that task arrived **59 minutes later** (`a97ff79c7`, the attended base-aware skill-link fix, which edited this very file at `:228`) and did not fold it in, because **a fold-into-next-edit gate names no owner and no instrument watches it**. Nothing was wrong with the judgement that the dispatch cost exceeded the fix; what failed is that the cure was keyed on an event with no subscriber. 💡 *A deferred fix whose trigger is "whoever next touches this file" is not scheduled — it is hoping. Either do it, or give it a gate something actually evaluates.*

🟢 **F-1596-2** (s1596 2026-08-09, CURED THIS FIRE — A DRAIN’S OWN MERGE CAN ROT A CITATION IN ITS OWN MASTER, AND ONLY THE POST-BOOKKEEPING BATTERY SEES IT). Non-blocking, cured in `cdef3885e`. ⚙️ **WHAT HAPPENED:** `tasks/lane-team-and-training.md` cited `e2e/milk-county-board.spec.ts:466` ("plain boot: posse chips rank within size, the field book counts hands, and a row watches its run") **twice, bare** — and the slice it dispatched **edits that very file**, rewriting the labels asserted on that line. `test:citations` went **FAIL — 1 citation cites a line with no recoverable test title**. ✓ **CURED the way the guard prescribes, additively (Retention Law):** the enclosing test title *"plain boot: posse chips rank within size, the field book counts hands, and a row watches its run"* is now quoted beside both citations; original wording untouched. Post-fix `NUMBER-ONLY 261→259`, `CARRIES-TITLE 255→257`, PASS. 🔑 **WHY THIS IS A CLASS AND NOT A TYPO:** a master’s citations point at the code the master is about to CHANGE. The coordinate is therefore **guaranteed** to be at risk from its own slice — this is [[your-own-cure-rots-the-coordinates-its-evidence-cites]] with the shortest possible fuse, author to rot inside one fire. ⚠️ **AND IT WAS INVISIBLE UNTIL THE LAST POSSIBLE MOMENT:** the master was authored attended-side at `7a564d8ad` and the drain gate ran on the MERGED TREE before the ledger commit — exactly the F-1300-4 blind spot. **The drain battery could not have caught it; only `test:ledger-guards` run AFTER the bookkeeping did.** 💡 *Reusable: when authoring a master, prefer citing a spec by TEST TITLE rather than by line — the line is the thing your own slice is about to move.* **GATE: none — closed by the patch.**
🟢 **F-1595-2 (s1595 2026-08-09, HIT AND CURED IN THE MANDATORY POST-HANDOFF BATTERY — THE HOUSE BANNER CONVENTION SILENTLY UN-DECLARES ANY DESK ITEM WHOSE ROW IT BANNERS).** Non-blocking, cured this commit, and filed because the convention is two fires old and WILL recur. ⚙️ **THE COLLISION:** the banner convention minted by F-1594-1 prepends `⛔ **CURED — DO NOT AUTHOR…**` immediately after a row’s leading marker, preserving the original wording behind it (Retention Law). But `scripts/desk-declaration-guard.mjs` requires a desk item’s declaring row to carry that item’s key **as its FIRST key, within the first 90 characters** — so a banner longer than ~85 chars **pushes the row’s own F-ID out of the declaring zone and the item silently becomes undeclared.** ✓ **MEASURED, not reasoned:** bannering `F-1364-1` — a live desk item (the AP-03/04/05 re-greenlight, *“ONE WORD unblocks THREE leaves”*) — took the guard to `undeclared: 1 · FAIL`. ⓘ **s1594 minted the convention and did not hit this, by luck alone: none of its four bannered rows (`F-1565-2`/`F-1515-2`/`F-1551-1`/`F-1549-2`) was a desk item.** The convention has therefore been latent-defective since birth and looked perfectly safe for a full fire. ➡️ **CURED — the fix is a keystroke and should become the convention: restate the row’s own key immediately after the marker, banner second** — `🟡 **F-1364-1** ⛔ **CURED — …**` — which satisfies the guard, keeps the banner prominent, and preserves the original wording verbatim behind both. Post-fix: `undeclared: 0 · PASS`. ⚠️ **AND THE ONLY REASON THIS WAS CAUGHT IS F-1300-4**, the law that runs `test:ledger-guards` as the LAST act *after* the bookkeeping commit: the guard SKIPs while an `ACTIVE` lock is on line 1, so it is structurally silent for a fire’s entire working life and speaks only once the handoff lands. **A fire that ran the battery only at its drain gate — the prescribed place — could not have seen this.** 💡 *Reusable shape: **a convention that edits the FRONT of a row is a convention that edits every position-sensitive guard’s input.** The banner and the declaring-key rule are each individually correct and were written by different fires for different reasons; the defect lives where one silently consumes the other’s budget. Same seam-between-two-correct-procedures class as [[F-1595-1]] — and found the same way, by running the thing afterwards instead of reasoning about it.*

🟢 **F-1595-1 (s1595 2026-08-09, MEASURED + CURED THIS FIRE (6 rows) + THE NARROW ARM BUILT AND MEASURED — THE HEADER-SCAN CLASS RE-ARMS ITSELF ONCE PER SHIPPING FIRE — BUT MY OWN "MISTAKE #8 ARMED" SEVERITY CLAIM WAS REFUTED BY PROBE IN THIS SAME FIRE, AND THE REFUTATION IS THE MORE USEFUL HALF).** Non-blocking. Discharges s1594’s (B) and (C). ✓ **(B) THE FOUR UNJUDGED F-1594-1 RESIDUE ROWS ARE JUDGED — 1 BANNERED, 3 CLEAR, each by probe not by prose.** `F-1364-1` **BANNERED** (cure `6d7f888f` confirmed an ancestor of main; `tasks/goals.json` carries the owner’s full conjunction + `DO NOT AUTHOR OR QUEUE` on both `ap-03-protocol` and `ap-04-teach`). `F-1368-1` **CLEAR** (`5570b457e` rebuilt `docs/MAP-CAMPAIGN-LEDGER.md`, `landmarks` column present). `F-1357-1` **CLEAR** (`BACKLOG.md:2948` reads `✅ RETIRED s1357`; its tail explicitly rules the remainder owner-gated, NOT fire-authorable). `F-1347-1` **CLEAR** (`f6af7eae` on main; its own tail states `GATE: none — closed by the patch`). 🔑 **THE DISCRIMINATOR, AND IT IS ONE WORD: `MEASURED` IS A VERB ABOUT THE INVESTIGATION, NOT ABOUT THE DISPOSITION.** The three clear rows lead with `CORRECTED` / `EXECUTED` / `PATCHED`; the one bannered row leads with `MEASURED` alone. s1594 hunted this class with a vocabulary of `CLOSED s<N>`/`ABSORBED` plus the marker and called its own pass-1 under-counting; the actual test is cheaper and sharper — **does the header zone contain ANY past-tense disposition verb?** A header that says only MEASURED has told the reader an investigation happened and nothing about what became of it. ⚠️ **(C) I BUILT s1594’S RECOMMENDED NARROW MERGE-HASH ARM, AND IN ITS RECOMMENDED FORM IT DOES NOT SURVIVE CONTACT WITH THE LEDGER — RECORDED SO NOBODY BUILDS IT TWICE.** `scripts/tmp-s1595-tail-closure-scan.mjs`, run over the live board: **1,353 F-ID rows · 503 carry an open-ish marker with no closure verb in the first 260 chars · 62 name a cure-at-hash in the tail · 61 of those hashes are ancestors of main.** **61 of 503 is a 12% red rate on correctly-written rows**, because a row legitimately stays open while citing the hash of a shipped CHILD or a half-landed cure. That is the exact fate s1594 predicted for the *judgement*-shaped guard (excused into uselessness, the `cross-engine` fate F-1460-1) — **and it applies equally to the arm s1594 recommended instead.** A narrower aim was needed, not a narrower vocabulary. ✅ **RE-AIMED AT THE SUB-CLASS WHERE THE QUESTION IS THREE FACTS AND NO JUDGEMENT — DISPATCH ROWS.** `scripts/tmp-s1595-dispatch-row-scan.mjs`. A dispatch row does not describe a problem, it **advertises a queueable master BY PATH**, so the conjunction is checkable: *header invites dispatch and declares no closure* **AND** *tail says shipped at a hash `git merge-base --is-ancestor` places on main* **AND** *the master file is still on disk and therefore still `cp`-able*. **322 rows cite a `tasks/*.md` path · 19 header-invites · 5 ARMED, and all five masters were still on disk.** ⚠️ **THE DENOMINATOR WAS WRONG ON FIRST PASS AND I VARIED THE PATTERN RATHER THAN TRUSTING IT** (a class-grep reports its hits and never its misses): the first regex required `**slug** \`tasks/x.md\`` and saw **15** rows, while `grep` for a backticked `tasks/*.md` path anywhere saw **322**. Keying on the cp-able PATH instead of the slug’s position took the armed count 3 → 5. 💥 **THE INJURY, WHICH IS WHY THIS IS NOT HYGIENE:** `f1591-1-frame-supply-cliff` advertises itself in its header as *“the fourth attempt at the still-open F-1587-2”* — and **F-1587-2 IS still open on the owner’s desk with five attempts against it.** A fire hunting that thread finds a 🔧 row naming a master by path, still on disk, header saying `FIRE-AUTHORED`, and reaches for **attempt 6 against an already-merged diff**. **s1594’s handoff warned *“do not manufacture an F-1587-2 attempt 6”* in PROSE, in a paragraph no dispatching fire is required to read; the row that invites it carried no warning at all.** ⓘ **The prose warning and the armed row had co-existed for a full fire.** 📈 **THE CLASS RE-ARMS ITSELF, WHICH IS THE FINDING’S REAL CONTENT: 3 OF THE 5 WERE MINTED IN THE LAST 5 FIRES.** This is not carelessness, it is the **standing output of the normal author→ship lifecycle**: a fire authors a master and writes a 🔧 row whose header says AWAITING DISPATCH; the drain later appends `SHIPPED … merged <hash>` to that row’s **TAIL**; nothing in the prescribed drain sequence ever revisits the **header**. So every shipped master leaves behind a row advertising its own re-dispatch, at a rate of roughly one per shipping fire. **A banner sweep cures instances; only a drain-time duty or a guard cures the class.** ➡️ **CURED THIS COMMIT (6 rows, house banner, original wording preserved VERBATIM behind each per the Retention Law): the 5 armed dispatch rows + `F-1364-1`.** Post-cure the scan reads **0 ARMED / 322**. ⚠️ **ONE KNOWN FALSE-POSITIVE CLASS, STATED RATHER THAN CURED BY CONCEALMENT: a hash cited as EVIDENCE PROVENANCE inside the verb window.** The `e3-voltage-socket` row fired on `merged \`0c4168a2\``, which is the provenance of the census DOC it cites, not the master shipping. **The verdict was still right** (that master shipped s1467 at `3ac90dd8…`) — so the arm errs toward flagging genuinely-stale rows — **but it was right for the wrong reason, and a guard whose reasoning is wrong 1 time in 5 must say so before anyone wires it.** That row is also the mildest of the six: its closure sits on the ✅ line immediately below it, i.e. inside a skim-reader’s index. Bannered anyway — an adjacent line is weak protection for a cp-able shipped master. 💡 *Reusable shape: **when a hygiene class keeps recurring, stop asking who was careless and ask which prescribed sequence MINTS it.** F-1594-1 read this as rows that were written badly. They were written correctly and then made false by a later, equally correct act — the drain appending closure to the tail. The defect lives in the SEAM between two correct procedures, which is why every individual author and every individual drain looks blameless and the class still grows once per fire.* ⛔ **REFUTED BY PROBE, IN THE SAME FIRE THAT CLAIMED IT — AND THE CORRECTION IS WRITTEN INTO THE HEADER ABOVE, NOT ONLY HERE, BECAUSE THAT IS THIS ROW’S OWN LAW.** I wrote *“Mistake #8 armed and loaded”* and then, while checking §0.1 of `/author-task` before authoring the mechanism, ran the gate on all five flagged masters: **`node scripts/drain-block-check.mjs --strict --queue <master>` returns rc=1 `⛔ ALREADY SHIPPED — DO NOT QUEUE` for all five of them** (`f1592-3`, `f1591-1`, `f1589-4`, `f1522-1-dispatch-lane-safety`, `e3-voltage-socket`), each naming its `mergeHash` and `refusal arm=status="merged"`. **A live mechanism already stands between the misleading row and the disaster**, and `/author-task` §5 makes running it the mandatory LAST act before any `cp`. 📉 **SO THE SEVERITY IS: a reading hazard and a wasted trip, NOT a re-derivation.** A fire misled by one of these headers spends its attention, reaches for the master, and is stopped at the gate. That is worth the six banners — it is not worth a new guard. 🚫 **THEREFORE NO MASTER WAS AUTHORED, AND THAT IS THE FINDING’S OPERATIVE OUTPUT.** The mechanism I was about to write would have duplicated `drain-block-check --queue` (the refusal) and `scripts/master-shipped-classifier.mjs` (the census — it already classifies all **988** masters, **575 SHIPPED**, and reports **63 NO-TRACE of which 63 self-declare DO NOT QUEUE → 0 candidates**). ⚠️ **HOW I NEARLY MISSED IT, WHICH IS THE REUSABLE PART: I reasoned from the INVITATION to the INJURY without ever checking what stood in between.** The row genuinely invites; the disaster is genuinely bad; and the step I never questioned was whether the path between them was open. **“Prove the lever” (F-1590-1) is normally applied to a CURE — this is the same duty applied to a HAZARD CLAIM: an invitation is not an injury until you have shown the path from one to the other is unguarded.** ⓘ The three prior fires in this thread (s1593 header-vs-row, s1594 tail-only closures, s1595 dispatch rows) each escalated the stated stakes and **none of them ran the gate**; the escalation was rhetorical, not measured. 💡 *And the honest summary of the class, now that it is measured end to end: **the ledger is misleading in a way that costs attention, and the factory is defended in a way that costs nothing.** Fix the prose; do not build a second lock for a door that already has one.*

🔧 **F-1594-1 (s1594 2026-08-09, FOUND BY THE CURE FOR F-1593-3 FAILING ITS OWN TEST ONE FIRE LATER — A CLOSURE WRITTEN INTO A ROW’S *TAIL* IS INVISIBLE TO THE HEADER-SCAN THAT EVERY DRY-BOARD AUDIT ACTUALLY PERFORMS).** Non-blocking, ledger hygiene, CURED THIS FIRE (4 rows). ✓ **MEASURED, not inferred.** s1593 closed the stale `F-1565-2` and filed **F-1593-3** to name the class, stating the rule verbatim: *"A finding is closed by an edit to ITS OWN row; a closure recorded only in a child row is invisible to anyone scanning for authorable work."* ⚠️ **Its own cure then satisfied that rule and still failed.** Read from the `52b45fa29` diff (**the DIFF, not the commit message** — Mistake #16): the closure went in as a paragraph appended to the **end** of F-1565-2’s row, and the row’s header was left byte-identical, still opening `🟡 **F-1565-2 (s1565 2026-08-08, OPEN, FIRE-AUTHORABLE …`. 🎯 **PROOF IT BITES, and it is not hypothetical: I hit it.** s1594 arrived on a dry board and ran the corrected (negation-aware) authorable sweep s1593’s handoff flagged as owed — `grep -n FIRE-AUTHORABLE | grep -vi "NOT FIRE-AUTHORABLE" | grep -i OPEN` — and **F-1565-2 came back as a live authoring candidate one fire after being closed.** The "real minutes of a dry-board audit" F-1593-3 measured as its own cost were paid again immediately, by the next reader, against the very row its cure had just touched. ➡️ **THE SHARPER CLASS: F-1593-3’s rule is true but NOT SUFFICIENT.** A row can be edited, edited *correctly*, and still advertise work that does not exist. **The reader’s index is the HEADER — the leading marker plus the first few hundred characters — because that is what `grep`, `cut -c1-260` and every human skim actually read.** A 4,000-character row’s tail is in nobody’s field of view. **A closure must reach the HEADER, not merely the row.** 📐 **SCOPE MEASURED ACROSS THE WHOLE LEDGER — AND MY FIRST MEASUREMENT UNDER-COUNTED ITS OWN CLASS, RECORDED RATHER THAN QUIETLY CORRECTED.** Pass 1 scanned all 3,514 lines for rows whose HEADER claims `OPEN`/`FIRE-AUTHORABLE` while the body records a closure: 3 hits, **2 real** — `F-1565-2`, and `F-1515-2` verified by COMMIT-PROBE (`8b853308dea09a5d6d8ed553cf91cf7de342a360` is an ancestor of main and its own message reads *"F-1515-1 cured, F-1515-2 latent-cured"*, so that row advertised a phantom authoring slot for **78 fires**) — and **1 false positive**, `F-1357-1`, whose matched `DISCHARGED` refers to a *quoted prerequisite* and whose header already declares `RETIRED`. **Reading each hit rather than trusting the grep is the only thing that separated them.** ⚠️ **PASS 1 WAS WRONG ABOUT THE DENOMINATOR IN TWO INDEPENDENT WAYS.** **(a)** Its closure vocabulary required a literal `CLOSED s<N>`, so it was blind to `ABSORBED s1552` / `CLOSED BY ABSORPTION`. **(b)** More fundamentally, **it keyed on header WORDS while half the real instances advertise through the MARKER alone** — a bare `🔧` is a live-corrective marker, and such a row invites authoring without containing the string `OPEN` or `FIRE-AUTHORABLE` anywhere in its header. Widening both axes found **2 more, both marker-shaped: `F-1551-1`** (`CLOSED BY ABSORPTION`; independently confirmed by CODE-PROBE — `assertWranglerVersion` is imported and called in all four consumers at `agent-seat-room.mjs:8`/`:68`, `test-accounts.mjs:6`/`:17`, `test-stats.mjs:6`/`:19`, `test-multiplayer.mjs:7`/`:38`) **and `F-1549-2`.** 💥 **`F-1549-2` IS THE INJURY THAT LIFTS THIS ABOVE HYGIENE.** Its header reads *"FIRE-AUTHORABLE NEXT PASS — … ALL FOUR PREMISES ARE NOW MEASURED SO THE MASTER IS ONE CLEAN PASS"*, while its own tail records that the s1552 run **STOPPED LAWFULLY WITH A ZERO DIFF** on an `npm install` **ERESOLVE** peer conflict (`wrangler@4.107.0` wants `@cloudflare/workers-types ^4.20260701.1`; `package.json:42` declares `^5.20260729.1`) and that the master is **NOT RE-QUEUEABLE AS WRITTEN**. **It was the single strongest candidate my own dry-board sweep surfaced, and only reading the tail stopped me queueing it** — a fire that trusted the header burns a lane run for zero diff, Mistake #8 exactly. ⓘ s1593 already KNEW this row was closed (its handoff says so) and still left the header advertising: knowing is not the same as writing it where the next reader looks. 💡 **That my class-grep caught a class and still under-counted it is the best available evidence FOR the class** — and it is the identical lesson `F-1551-1` recorded about itself: *"‘verified’ and ‘exhaustive’ are different properties, and only the first was checked."* 🔧 **CURED THIS FIRE by the established house mechanism, not a new one:** the `⛔ **CURED — DO NOT AUTHOR FROM THIS ROW …**` banner, **prepended after the leading marker** so the original wording survives verbatim (Retention Law — supersede, never rewrite). That convention already had **5 live instances**, 3 minted by F-1556-1’s s1556 sweep of this same shape. **4 rows bannered s1594: `F-1565-2`, `F-1515-2`, `F-1551-1`, `F-1549-2`** — each citing the probe that settled it. ⚠️ **RESIDUE NAMED HONESTLY RATHER THAN CLAIMED CLEAN.** The widened scan also surfaced 4 rows — `F-1368-1`, `F-1364-1`, `F-1357-1`, `F-1347-1` — whose headers read `MEASURED + CORRECTED` / `EXECUTED` / `PATCHED`, wording that arguably already tells a reader. **I did NOT read them line-by-line, so I am not claiming they are clean; I am naming them as UNJUDGED** so the next reader inherits a list rather than a false all-clear. 🚫 **DELIBERATELY NOT MECHANISED AS A GATE, now with a measured reason rather than an intuition.** s1593 declined to mechanise F-1593-3 because cross-row causality is a judgement and such a guard *"gets excused into uselessness — the `cross-engine` fate."* **Pass 1 supplies the number that argument lacked: 1 false positive in 3 hits.** A gate that reds a third of the time on correctly-written rows is one fires learn to wave through, and an excused red is worse than no test (F-1460-1, measured). ⓘ **What WOULD be