tasks/BACKLOG.md:890:✅ **F-1400-4 CLOSED s1479 (s1400, MEASURED — GATE ROBUSTNESS: one non-terminating sim hangs the WHOLE node battery, unbounded and undiagnosed).** `test:node-guards` invokes `node --test` with **--test-timeout=0**. A single sim that fails to terminate therefore hangs the entire 230-test battery with no bound, no diagnostic, and no partial report — a fire that hit this unattended would burn its whole window looking merely slow. Measured this fire twice on the baron-merged tree (12 min, then 300 s). ⚠️ **Second half, worth as much as the first:** `execFileSync`'s timeout SIGKILLs only its direct child, so the `node --test` runner and its spawned sim **survived as orphans** — one of them at 99.4% CPU with ppid 1 — and would have skewed every later timing-sensitive gate in the same shell, which is precisely the F-1270-1 failure mode. Both were re-verified by command string and reaped. **REC (cheap, mechanical, fire-authorable):** give the gr-sim node tests an explicit per-test timeout — twin-banks' own new test already sets `{ timeout: 45_000 }` and the baron's does not, so the convention exists and is merely unenforced. Folded into the twin-banks cure as scope item 6. Evidence reviews/e1-headless-bench-twin-banks-baron-s1400.md. ✅ **CLOSED s1479 — CURED IN-FIRE AT `565a145a5`, AND THE FIX IS NOT THE ONE THIS ROW RECOMMENDED.** `scripts/run-node-guards.mjs` now passes **`--test-timeout=300000`**, so all 268 tests are bounded instead of 3. 📐 **THE REC WAS THE WRONG SHAPE, measured before adopting it:** "give the gr-sim node tests an explicit per-test timeout" is **265 edits** (47 files / 268 `test()` declarations / **3** with a timeout) and it decays the moment anyone adds test 269 — a convention that must be re-applied by hand is not a bound. One default at the harness covers every existing test *and every future one*, which is the property the finding actually wanted. ⚠️ **THIS ROW'S OWN PREMISE ALSO DRIFTED AND IS RESTATED HONESTLY:** it says `test:node-guards` "invokes `node --test` with `--test-timeout=0`", but the script has since moved to `scripts/run-node-guards.mjs` (F-1409-1) and passes **no** timeout flag at all. The defect survived the rewrite because **node injects its own `--test-timeout=0` when the flag is absent** — measured s1479 by reading the child's `execArgv` — so the row was describing the *effect* correctly while naming a command that no longer existed. **Presence of the flag proves nothing; the VALUE is the whole discriminator**, and the guard is written that way. 🚫 **NOTHING WAS RAISED — the distinction F-1410-2 cares about:** a per-test `{ timeout }` overrides the CLI default, so the escort test's protected **120 s** and Twin Banks' 45 s are untouched; the other 265 move from unbounded to bounded, which is strictly tighter. **300 s is a CEILING, not a budget** — the slowest test in the real gating arrangement (fire shell, file concurrency **1**) is fixture-teardown at **34.2 s**, then 15.1 s and 14.9 s, so the bound sits at ~8.8× the measured worst and can only ever catch a hang. Sizing it tight would trade an unbounded hang for a flaky red, and **a red nobody trusts is the more expensive failure** (F-1460-1: five fires ignored a red wearing an excused label). ✅ **PROVEN BY EXECUTING THE VIOLATION PATH, never by a green** (s1299/s1300 standard): `scripts/node-guards-timeout.test.mjs` reds on all three mutations — pre-cure state and `=0` both → *"must be finite and positive, got --test-timeout=0"*, and `=3_600_000` → *"no longer protects a fire's window"* — with `run-node-guards.mjs` restored **byte-identical** (sha256 `5e4635543af4`). The override direction is proven the same way: at `--test-timeout=1000` an unbounded test was **cancelled at 1003 ms** while a `{ timeout: 10_000 }` sibling **passed at 3003 ms**. Probe retained at `logs/session-scratch/s1479-test-timeout-semantics.probe.mjs`. 🩺 Guard rooted in `test:node-guards` (unrooted would red `gate-caller-audit`); battery **305 tests / 302 pass / 0 fail / 3 skipped, rc=0, 150.0 s** (was 137.5 s; the guard's own cost is **4.3 s**), tsc clean. ⓘ **Scope stated honestly: this bounds the battery, it does not make any sim terminate** — F-1401-2's point that the bound must live inside the spinning loop still stands for the driver itself; this is the outer net that stops one hang from eating a fire's whole window. **GATE: none — closed.**
