tasks/BACKLOG.md:3525:- ✅ **F-1449-3 CLOSED s1450 `eea41d6e` — THE LAST KEYED SPRITE CARRYING THE F-1449-1 HALO IS CURED, AND THE DEFECT WAS BIGGER THAN THE FINDING SAID: THE BARON'S BANNER POLE HAS BEEN RENDERING AS A BRIGHT MAGENTA STICK.** s1449 named this *the cheapest win on the board* and deliberately left it for its own before/after QA rather than a drive-by; this is that QA. Re-extracted `assets/raw/prop-baron-banner.png` (1254×1254, fully opaque, `transparent=0`) with `--key ff00ff --size 384` against the cured `scripts/extract-alpha.mjs` (`c29040e7`). ⚠️ **The flags were MEASURED, not assumed** — s1449 explicitly warned *"do not assume `--size 384`; read how it was originally produced"*. `tasks/lane-b-baron-presence.md:9` records the original as bare `--key ff00ff`, and the extractor's `--size` **defaults to 1024** (`scripts/extract-alpha.mjs:52`), yet the shipped file measures **384×384** — so 384 is recovered from the artifact itself, not inherited from the drill-yard batch that happened to use the same number. 📏 **Measured before → after: key-magenta under transparency 99.96% → 0.00%**, and the **3,146** such pixels *directly touching visible art* — the halo source — go to **0**. ⭐ **CONFIRMED BY LOOKING (F-1446-1), which is the only reason any of this class was ever found:** at 6× (`artifacts/baron-banner-halo/zoom-pole-base.png`) the shipped sprite's staff is a **bright purple line** and every torn banner edge carries magenta fringing; the re-extraction renders the staff as **wood**. Silhouette, crossed-pickaxe sigil and canon are unchanged (`artifacts/baron-banner-halo/comparison-board.png`) — no firearms, no gore, nothing added. 🔺 **F-1450-4 OPEN — AND IT IS A CORRECTION TO A CLAIM I WOULD OTHERWISE HAVE INHERITED: THE CUT-OUT IS *NOT* BIT-IDENTICAL HERE, AND `bleedEdges` MUST NOT BE CREDITED OR BLAMED FOR THE DIFFERENCE.** s1449 measured, correctly, that the cure leaves transparent/opaque/partial counts and subject bboxes **identical** — but it measured that by re-extracting *the same batch twice with the same extractor*, differing only by the fix. This asset was last extracted at **s1064 (2026-07-08)**, weeks and many extractor revisions ago, so the observed **`partial` 1845 → 121** (a real drop in edge anti-aliasing) is **accumulated extractor drift across versions**, and the one-variable attribution does not hold across that gap. The honest statement is: *the halo is gone, measured and seen; the edge softness also changed, and this comparison cannot say which revision did it.* **A control that isolates it exists and is cheap — re-extract with the current extractor and `bleedEdges` stubbed — and was NOT run this fire.** Non-blocking (the sprite renders at roughly a 1-unit billboard, where downscaling dominates edge softness, and the halo it removes was unambiguously worse), but it must not be recorded as *identical, therefore safe*. **GATE: F-1450-4 closes when a same-extractor control attributes the `partial` delta, or an attended eye rules the edge-softness change a non-issue at play scale.** ✅ **CLOSED s1464 `941cbb6d` by gate branch 1 — see the standalone F-1450-4 row below; the premise of this paragraph ("accumulated extractor drift across versions") is REFUTED there.** ⓘ No gazette item was appended for this one: one was already owed and appended this fire for the crossings merge, and the banner is a wave-20 elite attachment few players will have seen — it belongs in the weekly roundup, not a headline.
tasks/BACKLOG.md:3530:- 🚀 **s1452 REFILL — lane-c DISPATCHED `f1452-1-fort-solidity-routes-long-static-blockers`, the F-BW-19 successor the last three handoffs named as the board's highest priority.** Leaf `f1452-1-fort-solidity-static-routing` registered `queued` in the master's own commit; `e1-baron-fort-solidity` stays **`stopped`** and `lane-baron-fort-solidity.md` is still **not to be re-queued unchanged** — its STOP was correct and this supersedes nothing. ⭐ **THE MECHANISM WAS RE-DERIVED BY READING THE CODE AT AUTHORING TIME RATHER THAN INHERITED FROM THE STOP'S PROSE, AND THE FIRST HYPOTHESIS WAS WRONG — recorded because the wrong one was plausible enough to have shipped.** The seam was initially placed at `BuildSystem.reservedFootprints`, on the strength of the collision getter admitting static bodies via `blocksRouting !== false`; reading `Game.ts` showed `reservedFootprints` is **megaprojects**, and that landmark colliders travel a *different* road entirely (`Terrain.landmarkBlockers()` ← `landmarkBlockersFor` ← the registry JSON). ✅ **The corrected, verified mechanism:** enemies **already** receive every registered landmark as a collider — `Game.ts` passes `[...this.buildSystem.palisadeBlockers, ...this.heroBlockers()]` and `heroBlockers()` spreads `Terrain.landmarkBlockers()` — and respond with local slide + depenetration in `resolveBlocker`; but enemy **routing** asks `BuildSystem.palisadeRoute`, which iterates `this.targets.palisade` **only**, as do all three of its private helpers. **A landmark is therefore structurally invisible to the route graph: a long fort wall is something an enemy can bump into but never plan around** — precisely the >1,400-fixed-tick stall the s1439 STOP measured, and the reason registry entries alone are a regression rather than a fix. The asymmetry sits one screen apart in the same file. ⚠️ **The constraint the master states up front so the runner cannot discover it the expensive way: `PalisadeRoute.blocker` is consumed as a wrecker GNAW TARGET** (`route.blocker` → `isBuildingValid` → HP chewing), while a `LandmarkBlocker` carries only id/x/z/halfX/halfZ with no `hp`, `family` or `position` — so a static body must yield a **waypoint without becoming gnawable**, and **whether enemies may ever damage a landmark fort is an OWNER/CANON question, explicitly OUT OF SCOPE and to be reported rather than decided.** The stall test must be proved load-bearing by a **manufactured red** (revert scope 1 → RED, restore → GREEN), since a green that passes both ways proves nothing about a stall. Dispatched in the **F-1424-3 order** — master + leaf + this row committed FIRST, lane refreshed SECOND, all **eight** citation keys proved `=1` on main **and again inside the refreshed lane** (F-1425-2), `cp` THIRD — and it carries the **FACTORY-CHURN EXCEPTION** (F-1407-1). **GATE: F-BW-19 closes when the owner can no longer walk through the fort walls AND an enemy crosses the same wall line without stalling, both measured.**
tasks/BACKLOG.md:3531:- 🔺 **F-1518-1 (s1518) — THE LANE SHELL AND THE FIRE SHELL DISAGREE ABOUT `test:node-guards`, AND THE LANE IS THE ONE THAT IS WRONG.** Measured on identical content this fire, which is what makes it a datum rather than an anecdote: the lane runner reported **354 tests / 352 pass / 2 fail** on Node **23.11.1**; the same battery, same commit, run by the drain under **26.4.0** (`.nvmrc`) is **354 / 351 pass / 0 fail / 3 skipped**, rc=0. The two lane reds are the known timeout-semantics split (`--test-timeout` bounded at FILE granularity on 23.x) and the extra skip is version-gated. ⚖️ **WHY THIS IS WORTH A ROW RATHER THAN A SHRUG: every lane run is gated against a knowingly different instrument than the one that admits its work.** Today that cost nothing — the runner correctly classified both reds as expected and shipped anyway — but it costs a fire the moment a *real* red hides among the excused ones, which is precisely the shape that let a stale Baron pin sit red for five fires under an excused `cross-engine` label (F-1460-1). A red nobody investigates is worse than no test, and a shell that manufactures two of them every run is a standing invitation to stop looking. ⓘ Non-blocking for the `f1510-3-revision-metadata-esm` drain (`0da47f37`): the fire shell is the gating instrument and it was green. **This is the sixth consecutive confirmation of [F-1507-1] and the first from a COMPLETED runner in six fires** — earlier confirmations were supervisor reruns, so this one measures the thing the lane actually does. **REC (owner, unchanged, one line): `~/.zshrc` — `nvm use 23` → `nvm use`. It is YOUR file, so no fire has touched it or will.** Related: [F-1507-1], [F-1460-1].
tasks/BACKLOG.md:3532:- ✅ **F-1518-2 (s1518) — `e1-baron-fort-solidity` FLIPPED `stopped` → `superseded`: A STALE LEAD THAT HAD ALREADY COST ONE FIRE'S ATTENTION, AND WOULD HAVE COST THE NEXT ONE A DUPLICATE MASTER.** s1517's handoff named this leaf as the board's one live authoring lead — *"its premise may have flipped ... but check whether the leaf is superseded before re-authoring"*. Measured this fire: **superseded, not flipped**, by `f1452-1` (`4ab48743`, merged attended 2026-08-05). ⚠️ **WHAT MADE IT LOOK LIVE IS A NOTE THAT WAS TRUE WHEN WRITTEN AND FALSE FOR EVERY READER SINCE.** The s1452 dispatch row states in bold that `e1-baron-fort-solidity` *"stays **`stopped`**"* and that the successor *"supersedes nothing"* — written at **dispatch** time, before `f1452-1` ran, let alone merged. Nothing updated it when the successor landed three days later, so a leaf whose work is fully shipped kept advertising itself as one of the board's 5 stopped leaves, with an explicit-looking instruction not to treat it as superseded. **A dispatch-time claim about a future merge has an expiry date, and this one had no mechanism to notice it passed.** ✅ **EVIDENCE, ALL RE-MEASURED ON TODAY'S MAIN RATHER THAN READ OUT OF THE SUCCESSOR'S REVIEW:** **(1)** all four registry gaps the STOP's five-map census named are **PRESENT** in `assets/pilots/map-rebuild-spike/landmark-collision-contract.json` — `active_headframe` (the-claim), `fortified_far_bank`, `seized_headframe`, `siege_line` (e1-baron); **(2)** `e2e/fort-landmark-collision.spec.ts` asserts the four solids block **every face** *and* **release embedded heroes**, at the exact body counts 7 / 5 / 1 / 1; **(3)** `e2e/fort-static-routing.spec.ts` asserts `longestStationary < 120` fixed ticks — against the **>1,400** this leaf's own STOP measured — plus arrival at target, which is exactly the never-trap clause the STOP refused to trade away; **(4)** both specs **RUN GREEN on main today**: desktop-chrome, `--workers=1`, rc=0, **2 passed in 46.1s**. The root cause this leaf named (`BuildSystem.palisadeRoute()` iterating player-built palisades only, so static landmark blockers never enter the route graph) is precisely the seam the successor edited: **+139/−31 in `src/systems/BuildSystem.ts`**. ⚖️ **TWO THINGS DELIBERATELY NOT DONE, because superseding a leaf is narrower than it feels.** **(a)** `tasks/lane-baron-fort-solidity.md` **remains not-to-be-re-queued-unchanged** — the STOP was correct then and is correct now; retiring the leaf does not rehabilitate the master. **(b) F-BW-19 is NOT closed by this fire.** Its four census gaps are discharged and re-verified, but the finding was born from an **owner gate walk** (*"I was also able to walk through its fortress walls"*), and an owner-walk finding closes on an owner re-walk, not on a fire's green — the same discipline that kept F-1510-3 open this same fire on a cure that shipped. **REC to the owner: on the next Baron gate walk, try to walk the fortress walls; if they hold, F-BW-19 closes.** 📉 **BOARD EFFECT: the goal tree's stopped count drops 5 → 4, and the one lead two consecutive handoffs pointed authoring at is now correctly marked done-by-another-slice.** Related: [F-1439-4], [F-1439-5], [F-1452-1].
tasks/BACKLOG.md:3545:- ✅ **F-1521-1 (s1521) — THE SECOND GHOST TELL IS GUARDED TOO, BUT ONLY AFTER PRICING IT CHANGED WHERE THE GUARD HAD TO LOOK (`scripts/stopped-leaf-supersession-guard.test.mjs`, wired into `test:ledger-guards`, now 87 tests).** s1520 shipped the FORWARD tell (a `stopped` leaf naming a `Successor:` that has shipped) and said plainly it caught only one of two, asking the next fire to **price the other before authoring it**. Priced, and the price was not what was expected. ⚠️ **THE TELL IS NOT IN `tasks/goals.json` AT ALL.** F-1518-2's ghost was an expired *dispatch-time claim* — grep `supersedes nothing`: it lives in `tasks/BACKLOG.md` and `STATUS.md`, PROSE LEDGERS. A parser for it would have to read the handoff corpus and understand future tense, which is the expensive guard s1520 feared. **It is deliberately NOT built.** 🔄 **INSTEAD THE DIRECTION WAS REVERSED, WHICH TURNED AN ENGLISH PROBLEM INTO A LOOKUP.** `f1452-1`'s own leaf names `e1-baron-fort-solidity` in its prose, so the predicate needs no tense and no NLP: **a SHIPPED leaf whose strings name a STOPPED leaf.** 📊 **REPLAYED, NOT ASSUMED — 884 revisions of `tasks/goals.json` since 2026-07-01: 18 distinct pairs ever fire, 11 RESOLVED on their own (the true-positive shape in hindsight), 7 stand today.** It names BOTH known ghosts: `f1452-1-fort-solidity-static-routing → e1-baron-fort-solidity` for **104 revisions, first firing 2026-08-05T18:34 — the moment f1452-1's leaf went merged, ~1.7 days and one wasted fire (s1517) before s1518 found it by hand**; and `f1441-2-crossings-keep-their-z → tb-stall-census` for 114, which is F-1519-1. 📏 **TWO NARROWINGS, BOTH FORCED BY MEASUREMENT AND BOTH LOAD-BEARING.** (a) A forward-loose draft (any non-shipped leaf naming any shipped `.md`) gave 8 candidates of which **6 sat on already-`superseded` leaves** — it mostly re-flags cured work. Dropped. (b) Including `blocked` leaves adds **6 standing false positives and zero real ghosts**; reading `blockClass` explains why — **all 6 blocked leaves are owner-gated** (5 `owner-fork` + `f1328-1` `disputed`, which fire.md §3.0 says to treat as owner-fork). So restricting to `stopped` is not a number-tuned heuristic, it is the statement that **only a fire's OWN hold is a fire's to re-examine**; widening it re-opens the rf-34 shape. A test locks that narrowness in. 🤝 **IT ACKNOWLEDGES RATHER THAN REDS, because 7 standing pairs on a board this old are not 7 defects — they are 7 questions already answered in prose.** A guard that reds on all of them every fire is the exact failure mode s1520 named (one fires learn to skip). A stopped leaf now carries `supersessionChecked`: an **object** from referent-id to the reason it does not supersede — an object, not an array, so an ack cannot be a silent tick. All **7 were walked and answered this fire from each referent's OWN title on today's board**, not inherited. 🔎 **ONE ACK IS NOT A CLEAN "NO", AND IT IS RECORDED AS SUCH:** `e2-rail-tough-only-bind` (`7ed00040`) **partially** discharged `eight-winds-wiring-e2-enemies` — it bound Rail Tough, one of that leaf's three E2 enemy slots, and its own title states *"Steam Wrecker + Coal Thief stay unbound (F-1193-3)"*. The leaf stays stopped (its STOP is Coal Thief's missing southwest row, an ART gap) **but its remaining scope is two slots, not three** — a fact no prior reader had written down, surfaced by the guard on its first run. ✅ **PROVEN BY MANUFACTURING THE RED, not by a green (s1299/s1300):** 5 fixture tests execute the violation, ack, blank-ack, array-ack, self-reference and owner-blocked-exclusion branches; the live test reddened on exactly the 7 predicted pairs **before** the acks were seeded and greened after. Full `test:ledger-guards` **87/87** (was 81), run through `node` because the bash allowlist refuses the npm name.
